 ### [Time-Sliced Pipelined Architecture](1435-time-sliced-pipelined-architecture.md)
-The Time-Sliced Pipelined Architecture is a hardware design methodology for volume rendering that achieves real-time performance by time-slicing functional blocks to allow the simultaneous processing of multiple tasks. It solves the latency and throughput bottlenecks of sequential rendering by overlapping execution stages, enabling continuous communication between hardware components and software control. It sets itself apart from standard pipelined approaches by explicitly reusing embedded sub-cores across different pipeline stages via a shared context memory, rather than dedicating fixed hardware to a single stage.
+The Time-Sliced Pipelined Architecture is a hardware design methodology for volume rendering systems that achieves real-time performance by time-slicing functional blocks to allow the simultaneous processing of multiple tasks. It solves the bottleneck of sequential hardware execution by overlapping pipeline stages, enabling continuous communication between hardware components and software interfaces. Unlike general-purpose DSP-based solutions that require multiple chips for similar volumes, this architecture integrates the entire rendering pipeline into a single system using reusable sub-cores, allowing for efficient layout and configuration based on specific processing requirements.
+
+### [Slice-by-Slice (Sheet Buffer) Splatting](2110-slice-by-slice-sheet-buffer-splatting.md)
+Slice-by-Slice (Sheet Buffer) Splatting is a volume rendering optimization that improves image quality over standard "splat-every-sample" compositing by correctly handling visibility through depth-ordered accumulation. It sets itself apart from its parent method, [Splatting](23-splatting.md), by organizing splats into 2D cache-sheets aligned with the volume axis most parallel to the image plane, compositing these sheets slice-by-slice in depth order, and then blending the result into the final image. Unlike [EWA Splatting](1069-ewa-splatting.md), which handles perspective distortion per-splat, this method primarily addresses the "sparkling" artifacts of list-based accumulation by ensuring correct occlusion, though it introduces its own "popping" artifacts if the view angle crosses specific thresholds.
 
 ### [Screen Quad Rendering](2435-screen-quad-rendering.md)
-Screen Quad Rendering is the final presentation stage in a volume rendering pipeline, responsible for transferring computed volume data from off-screen render targets to the default framebuffer or current context texture for display. It differs from standard blits by explicitly managing multiple draw buffers and allowing fragment shaders to sample intermediate textures for final compositing or debugging overlays.
+Screen Quad Rendering is the final compositing stage in a multi-pass volume rendering pipeline, responsible for displaying the accumulated volume data (stored in intermediate framebuffers) onto the screen. It solves the problem of transferring rendered results from off-screen render targets to the default framebuffer while allowing for post-processing effects such as cross-section slicing or debug overlays. It differs from sibling methods like [DirectXSplat Renderer API](8504-directxsplat-renderer-api.md) by explicitly separating the volume traversal/compositing logic from the final screen presentation, and from [Plug-and-Play Ray Marching and Rendering](981-plug-and-play-ray-marching-and-rendering.md) by relying on hardware-accelerated rasterization of a fullscreen primitive rather than software-based ray traversal for the final image generation.
 
